>tkae basket
(Taking "tkae" to mean "take".)
Taken.

>exmaine sketch
(Taking "exmaine" to mean "examine".)
It is a drawing, in your own clumsy pencil, of a girl with a way of tilting her
head as if the world were a question she meant to answer later. Fenchurch. You
have drawn her at least forty times and never once got the tilt right. She
vanished in the middle of a hyperspace jump, which is a thing that happens, and
which nobody has ever satisfactorily explained to you.

[The score has just gone up by two points.]

>/hint
[Hint 1 of 4] The shelf holds things worth looking at.

>/hint
[Hint 2 of 4] Look at both objects on the shelf.

>make hob the usual
Sandwiches are made at the sandwich hut; that is rather the point of it.

>e

The Village Green
A trampled circle of grass ringed by a dozen houses like your own. Smoke rises,
unhurried, from one or two chimneys. Your house is to the west, the sandwich hut
stands to the east, and a path runs north to the meadow where the occasional
ship puts down. A track drops away south towards the orchard slope, and two
small paths wander off to the southwest (the Reeve's lodge) and the southeast
(the smokehouse).

>e

The Sandwich Hut
A low hut with a wide shutter that folds down into a counter. This is where your
life happens. Shelves and a cool pit hold the ingredients, a chopping board sits
in the middle of the counter, and the knife hangs from its nail. The village
green is back to the west.

On the counter are a loaf of bread and a sandwich knife. In the cool pit are
some pickled roots. On the pantry shelf are some honey and some herb curd.

Hob leans on the counter with the air of a man who has been waiting since before
the sun. 

>make hob the usual
There is no cured meat in the cool pit at the moment. Dunna smokes the
village's, and she is not handing it over yet.

>make a sandwhich with honey and curd
(Taking "sandwhich" to mean "sandwich".)
(first taking the knife)
You slice, stack and press, with the unhurried competence of long practice. The
result is a sandwich of honey and herb curd, between two thick slabs of
Lamuellan bread.

>/hint
[Hint 1 of 4] Hob will want sourberries, and they grow on the slope south of the
green.

>ask hob abuot lunch
(Taking "abuot" to mean "about".)
"Same as always," says Hob. "Meat and sourberry, thick as you like. And no
pickle. I have views about pickle."

>who is hob?
(Taking a closer look at Hob.)
A broad, patient man with sawdust in his beard, and the air of someone who has
been waiting a very long time for lunch and hasn't minded.

>who was douglas adams?
[Out of the story: Douglas Adams (1952-2001) was an English writer and humorist,
born in Cambridge. He created The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy as a BBC
Radio 4 comedy that first aired in March 1978, then turned it into a series of
novels, five of them his own, ending with Mostly Harmless in 1992. He also wrote
the Dirk Gently novels, co-wrote Last Chance to See with the zoologist Mark
Carwardine, worked on Doctor Who as a writer and script editor, and co-wrote
Infocom's 1984 Hitchhiker's text adventure with Steve Meretzky. He died in
California in May 2001, at the age of 49. This game is an unofficial fan tribute
and has no connection with his estate.]

>what is a vogan?
(Taking "vogan" to mean "vogon".)
Vogons are the Galaxy's most efficient bureaucrats and its least attractive
people. They demolished the Earth to build a hyperspace bypass, and have been
completing the paperwork ever since. Their poetry is also said to be dreadful.
Nobody has asked you to confirm this.

>/difficulty easy
Easy: hints are unlimited, and the game will offer a nudge if you have been
stuck a while.

